## 3. From the library to the theorem

The library speaks about one chirotope and four split subsets. The
theorem quantifies over all configurations, midpoints, weights, and
splits. The bridge has five steps; each is either a short argument given
here in full or an exact computation named here and performed in this
directory.

### 3.1 Degenerate cases: fâ‚€ â‰¤ 40 without certificates

Vertices of a hull of a union are vertices of a part, so
fâ‚€(Q) â‰¤ fâ‚€(Z^a) + fâ‚€(Z^b). A 3-dimensional zonotope's vertex count
equals the chamber count of the central arrangement of its generators'
normal planes; five planes give at most 2(C(4,0)+C(4,1)+C(4,2)) = 22
chambers, attained only when the arrangement is simple. Hence:

- if U is **non-generic** (a vanishing determinant, a repeated or
  antiparallel direction, a zero direction â€” anything making the
  arrangement non-simple or smaller), each copy has â‰¤ 20 vertices and
  fâ‚€(Q) â‰¤ 40;
- if either copy **misses a generator** (a_i = 0 or b_i = 0, the cases
  outside the full-support chamber model), that copy has â‰¤ 14 vertices
  (four generic planes: 2(1+3+3) = 14) and fâ‚€(Q) â‰¤ 14 + 22 = 36.

Both are < 42, so from here on U is generic and both copies have full
support. One boundary remains inside the full-support regime: some
**a_i = b_i > 0**, where the residual Î´_i = a_i âˆ’ b_i vanishes and the
(w, s) normal form of Â§1 does not exist. It is absorbed by the
following lemma, which also supplies the genericity needed in Â§3.5.

**Lemma (generic-position perturbation).** Let Q, with parameters
(a, b, m) over a generic U, have fâ‚€(Q) = v. Then arbitrarily small
perturbations (aâ€², bâ€², mâ€²) exist whose zonoboxtope Qâ€² has fâ‚€(Qâ€²) â‰¥ v
and is in *strict generic position*: every residual aâ€²_i âˆ’ bâ€²_i is
nonzero, every side value on the twenty rays is nonzero, and no two
distinct candidate points coincide.

*Proof.* Every vertex q of Q is strictly exposed: some functional c has
âŸ¨c, qâŸ© exceeding âŸ¨c, pâŸ© by a positive margin for every candidate point
p âˆ‰ {q} (candidates = the 2â¶ sign points, finitely many). Candidates
vary continuously (polynomially) in (a, b, m), so after a small enough
perturbation the maximizer of c over the candidates is a candidate near
q, hence a vertex of Qâ€²; distinct vertices of Q stay separated and give
distinct vertices of Qâ€², so fâ‚€(Qâ€²) â‰¥ v. The conditions to avoid â€” a
vanishing residual, a vanishing side value, a coincidence of two
candidates that are not identical as polynomial maps â€” each cut a
proper algebraic subset of the parameter space (each is a nontrivial
polynomial equation: same-copy candidate differences are nonzero
because some differing sign contributes a nonzero u_i, and cross-copy
differences are nonzero polynomials even for matching sign patterns
because the a, b, m variables are independent). A finite union of
proper algebraic subsets has dense open complement, so the perturbation
can be chosen inside it. âˆŽ

Consequently every instance with fâ‚€ â‰¥ 43 yields, arbitrarily nearby,
a strict-generic-position instance with fâ‚€ â‰¥ 43 in the (w, s) normal
form with strict side signs. From here on we work with such instances.

### 3.2 Instance normalization: WLOG Ï‡(U) = Ï‡_ref and s âˆˆ four subsets

The group G = Sâ‚… â‹‰ {Â±1}âµ acts on configurations by
(Ï€, Îµ)Â·U = (Îµ_iÂ·u_{Ï€â»Â¹(i)})áµ¢. This action **preserves the polytope and
fâ‚€ exactly**: reorientation u_i â†¦ âˆ’u_i fixes each segment
m_i + w_i[âˆ’u_i, u_i] as a set, and relabeling permutes the segments
(with midpoints, weights, and split entries carried along, s â†¦ Ï€Â·s). On
chirotopes it acts by Ï‡ â†¦ (Ï€,Îµ)Â·Ï‡ as implemented in
`check_split_orbits.py`.

Two exact computations:

- **Single orbit** (`../check_om35_uniqueness.py`, stdlib): the uniform
  rank-3 chirotopes on 5 elements number exactly 384 and form a single
  G-orbit containing Ï‡_ref. So every generic instance can be moved by
  some g âˆˆ G â€” without changing its polytope â€” to an instance whose
  configuration has chirotope exactly Ï‡_ref.
- **Split orbits** (`check_split_orbits.py`, stdlib, exit 0): the
  stabilizer of Ï‡_ref has order 10 with faithful permutation image (the
  pentagon group Dâ‚…). Its orbits on split subsets are: {âˆ…}; all five
  1-subsets; two orbits of 2-subsets, represented by {0,1} and {0,2};
  and the complementary structure for k â‰¥ 3. Having landed at Ï‡_ref, a
  further stabilizer element (which fixes Ï‡_ref, hence keeps the
  configuration realizing Ï‡_ref) normalizes the split subset to one of
  **âˆ…, {0}, {0,1}, {0,2}** or a complement of one of these.

Complements reduce to the four representatives by the **global flip**.
Physically, swapping the roles of the two copies sends
(T, s, Ïƒ) â†¦ (âˆ’T, âˆ’s, âˆ’Ïƒ) with the geometric ray labels fixed; composing
with the antipodal ray relabeling (which restores the T coordinate)
gives the implemented involution (Ïƒ, s) â†¦ (flip Ïƒ, âˆ’s), where flip
negates Ïƒ *and* swaps the two rays of each class. It satisfies the
exact row identity
Row(flip Ïƒ, âˆ’s; class, ray) = Row(Ïƒ, s; class, âˆ’ray), so certificates
transport by the same ray swap; it is an involution and maps the
33,140-pattern valid set onto itself (verified exactly:
`check_transport.py` "FLIP OK", and independently re-checked in the
GPT review).

### 3.3 Certificate transport

Let g = (Ï€, Îµ) fix Ï‡_ref (a stabilizer element used in Â§3.2's split
normalization) or, more generally, map the instance's chirotope to
Ï‡_ref. The labeled system transforms covariantly:

- C_{lo,hi}(gÂ·U) = Ï„Â·C_{ij}(U) with Ï„ = Â±Îµ_loÂ·Îµ_hi (sign âˆ’ iff Ï€
  reverses the pair's order), so the ray structure of class (i,j) maps
  to the ray structure of class (Ï€(i),Ï€(j)) with a Ï„-twist; Ïƒ's twenty
  bits move accordingly;
- weight rows, split entries, and the D-variables permute by Ï€
  (D is reorientation-invariant: absolute determinants);
- the GP ideal and the coefficient-nonnegativity condition are
  G-invariant, so cell-wide certificates map to cell-wide certificates,
  and the 33,140 valid Ïƒ map bijectively onto the valid Ïƒ of the image
  chirotope (validity is defined by the chamber/side incidence, which is
  functorial in the chirotope).

These are exactly the claims a subtle error could hide in, so they are
**checked computationally, exactly**, in `check_transport.py`:
transported multipliers of sampled certificates from every bundle
(family, k=0, prefix sweeps, split02 sweep) are re-verified against an
independently built system for the literally-transformed integer
configuration gÂ·U_ints, over the full stabilizer and dozens of random
group elements â€” together with the bijection of VALID_BITS under all
ten stabilizer elements ("VALIDITY CLOSURE OK").

### 3.4 No strict 44 anywhere generic

Chain: a strict 44-vertex generic instance â†’ (3.2) an equal-fâ‚€ instance
at Ï‡_ref with split in one of the four representatives (or a
complement, reduced by the flip) â†’ its observed side pattern Ïƒ is valid
â†’ (Â§2) the library kills (Ïƒ, s) with an exact cell-wide certificate,
which (3.3) specializes to the instance's actual configuration â†’
contradiction with Gordan's theorem. So no generic instance attains 44
with strict side signs.

### 3.5 Parity, perturbation, and attainment: max = 42

- **No 43 and no non-strict 44:** suppose any instance (any parameters,
  U generic by Â§3.1) has fâ‚€ â‰¥ 43. The Lemma of Â§3.1 produces a nearby
  instance in strict generic position with fâ‚€ â‰¥ 43. In strict generic
  position the chamber-model count is literal â€” no cross-copy candidate
  coincidences can merge vertices â€” so fâ‚€ = 22 + #bicolored, and the
  bicolored count is even: the zero set of the support-function
  difference traces cycles in the bipartite chamber-adjacency graph
  (adjacency is bipartite because the chamber graph is dual to a
  great-circle arrangement on SÂ², 2-colorable by parity of the sign
  vector), and each bicolored chamber contributes exactly one segment â€”
  the zero plane meets a strictly convex open cone in a single
  connected sector, so no chamber is re-entered. Hence fâ‚€ is even and
  â‰¥ 43, i.e. fâ‚€ = 44 with all side signs strict â€” which Â§3.4 kills.
  So no instance reaches 43.
  (Parity argument verified: `../stage2b_gpt/REVIEW_stage2b_claude.md`.)
- **42 is attained:** `../cert_35_42.json` â€” an explicit rational
  42-vertex (3,5)-zonoboxtope, verified exactly by this repo's
  stdlib verifier (`../verify_c66_new_cases.py`) with strict-witness
  vertex confirmation, and independently by Fukuda's cddlib in exact GMP
  arithmetic (`../check_instances_cddlib.py`).

Together: **max fâ‚€(3,5) = 42.**

