407-The subtle difference lies in the possibility to take translated line segments.
408-Translation is allowed in (<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S2.E6" title="In 2 From Networks to Polytopes ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) but not in (<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S2.E5" title="In 2 From Networks to Polytopes ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
409-</div>
410-<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.p16">
411-<p class="ltx_p">To capture this distinction, we introduce classes of polytopes to be called ‘<em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">candidates</em>’.
412:A <math alttext="(d,n)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-<em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">zonoboxtope candidate</em> is a convex hull of two zonotopes in <math alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, each with <math alttext="n" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m3" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> generators,
413-where corresponding generators are parallel.
414:A <math alttext="d" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m4" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-<em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">boxtope candidate</em> is a <math alttext="(d,d)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m5" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,d)</annotation></semantics></math>-zonoboxtope candidate.
415:Finally, a <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math alttext="(d,n,m)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m6" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-maxout candidate</em> is the Minkowski sum of <math alttext="m" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m7" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> scaled <math alttext="(d,n)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p16.m8" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-zonoboxtope candidates.
416-Hence zonoboxtope (candidates) are projections of boxtope (candidates).
417-This generalizes the familiar fact that zonotopes are projections of cubes.</p>
