420-<section class="ltx_section" id="S3">
421-<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
422-<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Boxtopes</h2>
423-<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.p1">
--
426-In this section, we identify the <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maximal <math alttext="d" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.p1.m7" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-boxtopes</em>.
427-By this, we mean those <math alttext="d" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.p1.m8" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-boxtopes whose f-vector is maximal in each component.</p>
428-</div>
429-<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.p2">
430-<p class="ltx_p">An important ingredient in the proof is Proposition <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3 Boxtopes ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, in which we show that maximal <math alttext="d" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.p2.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-boxtopes with <math alttext="2^{d+1}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.p2.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices are <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cubical</em>.
431-A polytope <math alttext="P" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.p2.m3" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cubical</em> if all faces are combinatorially equivalent to cubes.
432-Cubicality is a recurring theme in this paper.
433-In Corollary <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 5.2. ‣ 5 Separating hypersurfaces ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>,
434:we show that a broad class of zonoboxtope candidates are cubical.
435-Finally in Section <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S7" title="7 Cubical structures in generic networks ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we demonstrate that good architectures of arbitrary depth yield cubical polytopes.</p>
436-</div>
437-<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.p3">
438-<p class="ltx_p">We now introduce two specific families of cubical polytopes, which give maximal boxtopes.
439-The first one exists in any dimension <math alttext="d" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.p3.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. It generalizes the polytope on the right in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
