441-<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
442-<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
--
621-</tr></tbody>
622-</table>
623-<p class="ltx_p">One checks, using computer algebra, that this is the unit ideal <math alttext="\langle 1\rangle" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle 1\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
624-So, it has no solutions.</p>
625-</div>
626-</div>
627-<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.p2">
628-<p class="ltx_p">In what follows, we fix the number <math alttext="\ell=2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=2</annotation></semantics></math> of layers and we write <math alttext="(d,n,m)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,n,m)</annotation></semantics></math> for the type.
629:Recall that a zonoboxtope candidate is the convex hull
630-of two zonotopes with the same <math alttext="n" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m3" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> zones in <math alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m4" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
631-Among these are zonoboxtopes, which actually come from networks
632-and are thus written as in (<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S4.E10" title="In Example 4.1 (𝑑=2). ‣ 4 Spaces of polytopes ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
633:A maxout candidate is the Minkowski sum of <math alttext="m" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m5" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> scaled <math alttext="(d,n)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m6" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-zonoboxtope candidates,
634-while a maxout polytope is the Minkowski sum of <math alttext="m" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m7" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> scaled <math alttext="(d,n)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.p2.m8" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-zonoboxtopes.</p>
635-</div>
636-<div class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition" id="S4.Thmtheorem2">
637-<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem">
638-<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span>
