  18: \title[The maximum vertex number of a $(3,5)$-zonoboxtope is 42]{The maximum number of vertices\\ of a $(3,5)$-zonoboxtope is $42$}
  19: \author{Reuel Lee}
  20: \address{}
  21: \email{reuellee@gmail.com}
  22: 
  23: \date{July 31, 2026}
  24: 
  25: \begin{abstract}
  26: A $(d,n)$-zonoboxtope is a maxout polytope of type $(d,n,1)$: the convex
  27: hull of a union of two weighted Minkowski sums of a common family of $n$
  28: segments in $\RR^d$. Balakin, Cox, Loho, and Sturmfels
  29: (arXiv:2509.21286) prove that a $(3,5)$-zonoboxtope has at most $44$
  30: vertices, assert that this bound is tight, and conjecture
  31: $4\sum_{k\le 2}\binom{n-1}{k}$ as the maximal vertex number of
  32: $(3,n)$-zonoboxtopes for all odd $n$. We prove that the maximum at
  33: $(d,n)=(3,5)$ is exactly $42$: the bound $44$ is not attained, the
  34: tightness assertion fails, and the odd-$n$ case of the conjecture fails
  35: at its first instance beyond $n=3$. The proof is computer-assisted and
  36: fully certified: a symmetry reduction to a single oriented-matroid cell
  37: and four split representatives, followed by $132{,}560$ exact Gordan
  38: certificates whose multipliers are polynomial in the maximal minors of
  39: the configuration, verified modulo the Grassmann--Pl\"ucker ideal in
  40: exact rational arithmetic. An explicit rational $42$-vertex instance is
  41: included, together with a stdlib-only verifier, as ancillary files. In
  42: the positive direction we certify that the conjectured values are
  43: attained at $(3,8)$ and $(4,6)$, the latter resolving that case
  44: completely.
  45: \end{abstract}
  46: 
  47: \begin{document}
  48: \maketitle
  49: 
  50: \section{Introduction}
  51: 
  52: Maxout polytopes were introduced by Balakin, Cox, Loho, and Sturmfels
  53: \cite{BCLS} as the polytopes computed by feedforward neural networks
  54: with maxout activation and nonnegative weights after the first layer.
  55: The first interesting class beyond zonotopes are the polytopes of type
  56: $(d,n,1)$, called \emph{$(d,n)$-zonoboxtopes} in \cite[\S 6]{BCLS}:
  57: polytopes of the form
  58: \begin{equation}\label{eq:zbt}
  59:   Q \;=\; \conv\Bigl(\;\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i I_i \;\cup\; \sum_{i=1}^{n} b_i I_i\Bigr)
  60:   \;\subset\; \RR^d,
  61: \end{equation}
  62: where $I_1,\dots,I_n$ are segments and $a,b\in\RR_{\ge 0}^n$. Writing
  63: $I_i = m_i + [-u_i,u_i]$ with midpoint $m_i\in\RR^d$ and direction
  64: $u_i\in\RR^d$, the parameters are $(u_i, m_i, a, b)$.
  65: 
  66: For $d=3$, \cite[Proposition 6.5]{BCLS} states that a
  67: $(3,n)$-zonoboxtope has at most $16, 26, 44, 60$ vertices for
  68: $n=3,4,5,6$, and that these bounds are tight. The upper bounds are
  69: proven there by a depth-first search over the combinatorial types of
  70: three-dimensional zonotopes; the tightness by extremal examples found
  71: by sampling. Part 1 of \cite[Conjecture 6.6]{BCLS} proposes, for the
  72: maximal vertex number of a $(3,n,1)$-maxout polytope,
  73: \[
  74:   4\sum_{k=0}^{2}\binom{n-1}{k} \ \text{ if } n \text{ is odd},
  75:   \qquad
  76:   4\sum_{k=0}^{2}\binom{n-1}{k} - (n-2) \ \text{ if } n \text{ is even},
  77: \]
  78: which equals $44$ at $n=5$. Our main result is:
  79: 
  80: \begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
  81: The maximum number of vertices of a $(3,5)$-zonoboxtope is $42$.
  82: \end{theorem}
  83: 
  84: Consequently the upper bound $44$ of \cite[Proposition 6.5]{BCLS} is
  85: correct but not attained: its tightness assertion fails at $n=5$, and
  86: the odd-$n$ case of part 1 of \cite[Conjecture 6.6]{BCLS} fails at its
  87: first instance beyond $n=3$. In particular no sampled instance can
  88: attain $44$ at $n=5$; since our formalization reproduces every other
  89: vertex number reported in \cite[\S 6]{BCLS}, we suspect a
  90: floating-point vertex-count artifact in the tightness verification of
  91: the sampled $n=5$ example rather than any definitional mismatch, and
  92: the ancillary verifier of this note can check any explicit instance in
  93: seconds. Following \cite[Remark 6.7]{BCLS}, our upper bound is in fact
  94: proven for the wider family of \emph{zonoboxtope candidates} (two
  95: zonotopes with pairwise parallel generators and an arbitrary relative
  96: translation --- the translation parameter $T$ below is unconstrained),
  97: while the $42$-vertex witness is a genuine zonoboxtope; so the maximum
  98: is $42$ for both families. The attainment half of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is
  99: the following explicit instance, whose vertex count is verified in
 100: exact rational arithmetic by a Python-stdlib program included with this
 101: note (Section~\ref{sec:verification}).
 102: 
 103: \begin{proposition}\label{prop:instance}
 104: Let $u_1,\dots,u_5$ be the rows of
 105: \[
 106: U=\begin{pmatrix}
 107: -19/26 & 1/30 & -15/22\\
 108: 0 & -1 & 1/16\\
 109: 1/19 & 20/23 & -1/2\\
 110: 10/21 & -6/7 & -6/29\\
 111: 6/19 & 4/27 & 15/16
 112: \end{pmatrix},
 113: \]
 114: let $m_1 = (-1/7,\, 17/28,\, 1/12)$ and $m_2=\dots=m_5=0$, and let
 115: \[
 116: a = \bigl(\tfrac{47}{25}, \tfrac{49}{20}, \tfrac94, \tfrac{14}{17}, \tfrac{23}{18}\bigr),
 117: \qquad
 118: b = \bigl(\tfrac{16}{17}, \tfrac{27}{22}, \tfrac98, \tfrac{28}{17}, \tfrac{23}{9}\bigr).
 119: \]
 120: Then the $(3,5)$-zonoboxtope \eqref{eq:zbt} has exactly $42$ vertices.
 121: \end{proposition}
 122: 
 123: The substance of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is the upper bound. It is a
 124: computer-assisted proof in the certificate tradition: the mathematical
 125: content is carried by exact, independently checkable certificates --- a
 126: library of $132{,}560$ Gordan infeasibility certificates valid across
 127: an entire oriented-matroid cell, plus small exact computations for the
 128: symmetry reduction --- while search heuristics (floating-point linear
 129: programming, sampling) were used only to \emph{find} certificates,
 130: never to justify a claim. Section~\ref{sec:architecture} describes the
 131: proof; Section~\ref{sec:verification} describes how to verify it. The
 132: complete certificate library, all generating and checking programs, and
 133: a detailed prose account are in the public repository
 134: \begin{center}
 135: \url{https://github.com/reuellee/finite-certificates}
 136: \end{center}
 137: (directory \texttt{ai/maxout}; the capstone document
 138: \texttt{ai/maxout/capstone/CAPSTONE.md} gives the full argument with
 139: file-level pointers).
 140: 
 141: In the positive direction, the same exact-verification harness confirms
 142: two further values asserted or conjectured in \cite{BCLS}:
 143: 
 144: \begin{proposition}\label{prop:positive}
 145: There exist a $(3,8)$-zonoboxtope with $110$ vertices and a
 146: $(4,6)$-zonoboxtope with $104$ vertices, both certified in exact
 147: arithmetic. In particular the even-$n$ value $110$ of
 148: \cite[Conjecture 6.6]{BCLS} is attained at $n=8$, and the case $(4,6)$
 149: of the conjecture is resolved completely: the maximum there is exactly
 150: $104$, since $104$ equals twice the maximal vertex number of a
 151: $4$-dimensional zonotope with $6$ generic generators.
 152: \end{proposition}
 153: 
 154: We also certify $58$ vertices at $(4,5)$ and $84$ at $(3,7)$
 155: (conjectured maxima: $60$ and $88$). Extensive complete-per-instance
 156: searches found no more; whether the odd-case deficit $2$ persists for
 157: all odd $n>3$ is an open question we do not settle here.
 158: 
 159: \section{The proof}\label{sec:architecture}
 160: 
 161: Throughout, $(d,n)=(3,5)$. We outline the chain; every step is either a
 162: short argument reproduced here or an exact computation named here and
 163: shipped in the repository.
 164: 
 165: \subsection{Degenerate cases}\label{sec:degenerate}
 166: Every vertex of a hull of a union \eqref{eq:zbt} is a vertex of one of
 167: the two zonotopes, so $f_0(Q)\le f_0(Z^a)+f_0(Z^b)$. The vertex count
 168: of a $3$-dimensional zonotope equals the chamber count of the central
 169: plane arrangement dual to its generators, which is at most
 170: $2\bigl(\binom40+\binom41+\binom42\bigr)=22$ for five planes, with
 171: equality iff the arrangement is simple. Hence if the direction
 172: configuration $U=(u_1,\dots,u_5)$ is \emph{not} generic (some $3\times3$
 173: minor vanishes), each copy has at most $20$ vertices and $f_0\le 40$;
 174: and if some $a_i=0$ or $b_i=0$, that copy has at most
 175: $2(1+3+3)=14$ vertices and $f_0\le 36$. Both are below $42$, so we may
 176: assume $U$ generic and $a,b>0$.
 177: 
 178: \subsection{The chamber model and the target systems}
 179: For generic $U$ and $a,b>0$ the two zonotopes are normally equivalent,
 180: and (specializing the framework of \cite[\S\S 5--6]{BCLS} to two
 181: normally equivalent zonotopes)
 182: $f_0(Q) = \#\text{chambers} + \#\text{bicolored chambers}$, where a
 183: chamber of the arrangement $\{u_i^\perp\}$ is \emph{bicolored} when it
 184: contains support vectors of both copies. With $22$ chambers this gives
 185: $f_0\le 44$, with equality iff every chamber is bicolored.
 186: 
 187: Write $\delta_i = a_i - b_i$, let $s_i=\sign\delta_i\in\{\pm1\}$ be the
 188: \emph{split} and (after a normalization removing square roots)
 189: $w_i>0$ the residual weight; all midpoint freedom enters through a
 190: single vector $T\in\RR^3$ (an explicit linear combination of the
 191: $m_i$).
 192: The all-chambers-bicolored condition becomes a strict linear
 193: feasibility system in $x=(T,w)\in\RR^3\times\RR^5$: for each unordered
 194: pair $i<j$, the two antipodal rays $\pm C_{ij}$, $C_{ij}=u_i\times u_j$,
 195: each carry a sign $\sigma_{ij,\pm}\in\{\pm1\}$, and the system is
 196: \begin{equation}\label{eq:system}
 197:   \sigma_{ij,\pm}\Bigl(\pm\langle C_{ij}, T\rangle
 198:   + \sum_{t\notin\{i,j\}} s_t\, D_{tij}\, w_t\Bigr) > 0
 199:   \quad (20 \text{ rows}), \qquad w_t>0 \quad (5 \text{ rows}),
 200: \end{equation}
 201: where $D_{tij}=\lvert\det(u_t,u_i,u_j)\rvert$. Not every sign pattern
 202: $\sigma\in\{\pm1\}^{20}$ is consistent with all $22$ chambers seeing
 203: both signs: the chamber/ray incidence, a combinatorial invariant of the
 204: chirotope of $U$, admits exactly $33{,}140$ \emph{valid} patterns
 205: (three independent enumerations agree). A $(3,5)$-zonoboxtope with
 206: $44$ vertices and strict side signs therefore yields a valid $\sigma$,
 207: a split $s$, and a strictly feasible system \eqref{eq:system}.
 208: 
 209: \subsection{Cell-wide Gordan certificates}\label{sec:certs}
 210: A vector $y\ge 0$, $y\ne 0$ with $B^{\mathsf T}y=0$, where $B$ is the
 211: $25\times 8$ coefficient matrix of \eqref{eq:system}, proves strict
 212: infeasibility (Gordan's theorem). We construct such certificates
 213: \emph{cell-wide}: the $25$ multipliers are polynomials in the ten
 214: quantities $D_{tij}$ --- side multipliers of degree $\le 2$, weight
 215: multipliers of degree $\le 3$, all coefficients nonnegative, at least
 216: one positive --- such that $B^{\mathsf T}y=0$ holds \emph{identically
 217: modulo the Grassmann--Pl\"ucker ideal} given the chirotope's signs.
 218: Such a certificate specializes to a valid Gordan vector at
 219: \emph{every} configuration realizing the chirotope: on a generic
 220: configuration every $D_{tij}>0$, so nonnegative coefficients give
 221: $y\ge0$ and the positive coefficient gives $y \neq 0$.
 222: 
 223: Two mechanisms produce the library. If some pair class $(i,j)$ has
 224: equal ray signs $\sigma^*$ with $\sigma^* s_t=-1$ for all
 225: $t\notin\{i,j\}$, the closed form $y_{ij,\pm}=1$,
 226: $y_{w,t}=2D_{tij}$ is a certificate with no Grassmann--Pl\"ucker
 227: reduction needed (and this \emph{single-class family} is provably the
 228: complete coefficientwise-positive mechanism). All remaining systems are
 229: killed by explicit degree-$(2,3)$ certificates found by exact kernel
 230: computations in the quotient ring; typical supports have four terms.
 231: Deliberately impossible control systems (``canaries'') were wired into
 232: every search and correctly rejected throughout.
 233: 
 234: \subsection{Symmetry: one cell, four splits}\label{sec:symmetry}
 235: The group $G=S_5\ltimes\{\pm1\}^5$ acts on configurations by
 236: $u_i \mapsto \varepsilon_i u_{\pi^{-1}(i)}$. The action preserves the
 237: polytope: reorientation fixes each segment
 238: $m_i+w_i[-u_i,u_i]$ as a set, and relabeling renames segments (carrying
 239: $m, a, b, s$ along). Two exact computations reduce the quantifiers:
 240: 
 241: \begin{itemize}
 242: \item[(i)] The uniform rank-$3$ chirotopes on $5$ elements number
 243: exactly $384$ and form a \emph{single} $G$-orbit
 244: (all rank-$3$ oriented matroids on $\le 8$ elements are realizable, so
 245: there are no other cells). Hence every generic instance moves, without
 246: changing its polytope, to an instance whose configuration realizes one
 247: fixed reference chirotope $\chi_{\mathrm{ref}}$ --- that of the integer
 248: configuration
 249: $U_{\mathrm{ref}}$ with rows
 250: $(-6,-13,18)$, $(-9,-12,8)$, $(-13,-4,16)$, $(4,-19,-8)$,
 251: $(16,15,-12)$.
 252: \item[(ii)] The stabilizer of $\chi_{\mathrm{ref}}$ in $G$ has order
 253: $10$ and faithful permutation image the dihedral group $D_5$ (the
 254: automorphism group of the pentagon). Its orbits on split subsets
 255: $A=\{t: s_t=+1\}$ are: $\{\emptyset\}$; all five singletons; \emph{two}
 256: orbits of $2$-subsets, represented by $\{1,2\}$ and $\{1,3\}$; and
 257: complements. Swapping the two copies reduces complements via an exact
 258: row correspondence (the \emph{flip}). Hence every instance normalizes
 259: to $\chi_{\mathrm{ref}}$ with split subset
 260: $\emptyset$, $\{1\}$, $\{1,2\}$, or $\{1,3\}$.
 261: \end{itemize}
 262: 
 263: We emphasize (ii): treating splits ``by size'' is \emph{not} sound ---
 264: the two orbits of $2$-subsets are genuinely inequivalent --- and an
 265: earlier version of our own library covered only one of them. The gap
 266: was found while writing out the transport argument and closed by a
 267: fourth sweep; we flag it because size-based split reductions look
 268: innocuous and are wrong.
 269: 
 270: The certificate library consists of an exact cell-wide certificate for
 271: each of the $33{,}140$ valid patterns at each of the four split
 272: representatives: $132{,}560$ certificates in total, of which $74{,}923$
 273: are closed-form single-class and $57{,}637$ are explicit
 274: quotient-ring certificates. Certificates transport along $G$
 275: covariantly (ray twisting by
 276: $\tau=\pm\varepsilon\varepsilon$, permutation of the $D$-variables,
 277: bijection of valid patterns); the transport bookkeeping is verified
 278: exactly on thousands of transported certificates against independently
 279: built systems, and the validity enumeration is verified to be preserved
 280: by the full stabilizer.
 281: 
 282: \subsection{Genericity, parity, and the endgame}\label{sec:endgame}
 283: Two boundary issues remain: the normal form requires all
 284: $\delta_i\ne 0$, and the chamber-model count requires no accidental
 285: coincidence among the $2^{6}$ candidate points. Both are handled by a
 286: perturbation lemma: every vertex of $Q$ is strictly exposed among the
 287: candidate points, and candidates vary polynomially in $(a,b,m)$, so
 288: all vertices persist under small parameter perturbations; the excluded
 289: loci (a vanishing residual, a vanishing side value, a coincidence of
 290: candidates) are finitely many proper algebraic subsets, whose union
 291: misses arbitrarily nearby parameters. Hence any instance with
 292: $f_0\ge 43$ yields a nearby \emph{strict generic position} instance
 293: with $f_0\ge 43$.
 294: 
 295: In strict generic position the count
 296: $f_0 = 22 + \#\text{bicolored}$ is literal, and the bicolored count is
 297: even: the zero set of the support-function difference crosses chamber
 298: walls transversally, meets each strictly convex chamber cone in a
 299: single sector, and traces cycles in the chamber-adjacency graph, which
 300: is bipartite (the chambers of a central simple arrangement of five
 301: planes $2$-color by sign-vector parity). So $f_0\ge 43$ forces
 302: $f_0=44$ with all side signs strict --- producing a valid pattern and a
 303: strictly feasible system \eqref{eq:system} at some realization of
 304: $\chi_{\mathrm{ref}}$ with one of the four split representatives, which
 305: the certificate library kills. Hence $f_0\le 42$, and
 306: Proposition~\ref{prop:instance} completes Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. \qed
 307: 
 308: \section{Verification}\label{sec:verification}
 309: 
 310: \subsection*{Ancillary files}
 311: The ancillary files of this note contain the five instance certificates
 312: (\texttt{cert\_35\_42.json}, \texttt{cert\_38\_110.json},
 313: \texttt{cert\_46\_104.json}, \texttt{cert\_45\_58.json},
 314: \texttt{cert\_37\_84.json}) and the stdlib-only verifier
 315: \texttt{verify\_c66\_new\_cases.py} ($\sim$2 seconds), which proves in
 316: exact \texttt{Fraction} arithmetic that each named instance has
 317: \emph{exactly} the claimed vertex count: all $2^{n+1}$ candidate sign
 318: points are pairwise distinct, each claimed vertex carries a strict
 319: witness direction, and each non-vertex carries an explicit convex
 320: combination. This suffices to verify Propositions~\ref{prop:instance}
 321: and~\ref{prop:positive} independently of everything else.
 322: 
 323: \subsection*{The upper-bound library}
 324: From the repository, directory \texttt{ai/maxout}:
 325: \texttt{check\_om35\_uniqueness.py} (the $384$-chirotope single-orbit
 326: computation; stdlib), \texttt{capstone/check\_split\_orbits.py} (the
 327: stabilizer and split-orbit accounting; stdlib),
 328: \texttt{capstone/check\_transport.py} (exact transport verification),
 329: and \texttt{stage2c2\_gpt/audit\_all\_certificates.py} --- a single
 330: standalone audit that re-verifies \emph{every} certificate in the
 331: library: coefficient nonnegativity, all quotient-ring identities
 332: re-derived modulo the Grassmann--Pl\"ucker ideal in exact rational
 333: arithmetic, and specialization at $U_{\mathrm{ref}}$ against an
 334: independently written row builder ($132{,}681$ checks including
 335: redundant coverage; zero failures; about $20$ minutes). The
 336: generation-side programs, sweep artifacts, and a complete prose account
 337: with erratum history are in the same directory.
 338: 
 339: \subsection*{Provenance}
 340: This result was produced in a multi-model AI research pipeline: search,
 341: certificate generation, and drafts of the arguments were carried out by
 342: large language models (GPT-5.6, Gemini 3.1 Pro, Claude) directing exact
 343: computer algebra, with adversarial cross-review between models,
 344: deliberately-impossible canary controls in every certificate search,
 345: and independent re-verification of every serialized object. No
 346: mathematical claim in this note rests on model output: the upper bound
 347: rests on the audited certificate library and the short arguments of
 348: Section~\ref{sec:architecture}, and the attainment on the ancillary
 349: stdlib verification. The author directed the program and takes
 350: responsibility for the claims.
 351: 
 352: \subsection*{Acknowledgments}
 353: I thank the authors of \cite{BCLS} for a beautiful paper. Any explicit
 354: $44$-vertex $(3,5)$-instance would contradict Theorem~\ref{thm:main}
 355: and could be checked in seconds by the ancillary verifier; I would be
 356: grateful for any such communication.
