1605-</div>
1606-</div>
1607-<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.p17">
1608-<p class="ltx_p">We apply this corollary to bound the vertex numbers of zonoboxtopes in dimension three.</p>
1609-</div>
1610-<div class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition" id="S6.Thmtheorem5">
1611-<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem">
1612:<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.5</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span>
1613-</h6>
1614-<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1">
1615-<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <math alttext="(3,n)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-zonoboxtope has at most <math alttext="16,26,44,60" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>44</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16,26,44,60</annotation></semantics></math> vertices
1616-for <math alttext="n=3,4,5,6" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3,4,5,6</annotation></semantics></math>. These bounds are tight.</span></p>
1617-</div>
1618-</div>
1619-<div class="ltx_proof">
1620-<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
1621-<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.p18">
1622-<p class="ltx_p">For fixed <math alttext="n" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, there are finitely many combinatorial types of three-dimensional zonotopes with <math alttext="n" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> generators.
1623:For each type <math alttext="Z" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m3" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform a depth-first search that finds a valid bicoloring of <math alttext="G(Z^{\ast})" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m4" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">â€‹</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>âˆ—</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(Z^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> with at most <math alttext="m" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m5" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> monocolored facets, or confirms that no such bicoloring exists.
1624-By Corollary <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S6.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 6.4. â€£ 6 Constructing extremal maxout polytopes â€£ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>, the smallest <math alttext="m" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m6" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for which a valid bicoloring exists gives an upper bound on the maximum number of vertices of
1625-the convex hull of two normally equivalent zonotopes which are combinatorially equivalent to <math alttext="Z" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m7" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
1626-Taking the maximum over all combinatorial types in with <math alttext="n" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p18.m8" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> generators gives upper bound for all
1627:zonoboxtope candidates.</p>
1628-</div>
1629-<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.p19">
1630-<p class="ltx_p">We prove that this bound is tight by exhibiting extremal zonoboxtopes.
1631:These were found by sampling, using a strategy that
1632-is inspired by Lemma <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2. â€£ 6 Constructing extremal maxout polytopes â€£ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> and the extremal examples in Theorem <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. â€£ 6 Constructing extremal maxout polytopes â€£ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>.
1633-We randomly pick line segments <math alttext="I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p19.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">â€¦</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1},\ldots,I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with endpoints uniformly randomly sampled from the unit sphere in <math alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p19.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>â„</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math alttext="a_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,a_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}^{\prime},b_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1}^{\prime},\ldots,b_{n}^{\prime}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p19.m3" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>â€²</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">â€¦</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">âŒŠ</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">âŒ‹</mo></mrow><mo>â€²</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">âŒŠ</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">âŒ‹</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>â€²</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">â€¦</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>â€²</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,a_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}^{\prime},b_{\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1}^{\prime},\ldots,b_{n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly randomly sampled from <math alttext="[0,1]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p19.m4" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
1634:Using only <math alttext="1000" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p19.m5" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> samples for each <math alttext="n=3,4,5,6" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.p19.m6" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3,4,5,6</annotation></semantics></math>, this method succeeds.
1635-âˆŽ</p>
1636-</div>
1637-</div>
