41-</div>
42-<div class="ltx_abstract">
43-<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
44-<p class="ltx_p">Maxout polytopes are defined by
45:feedforward neural networks with maxout activation function and non-negative weights after the first layer.
46-We characterize the parameter spaces and extremal f-vectors of maxout polytopes for shallow networks,
47-and we study the separating hypersurfaces which arise when a layer is added to
48-the network.
49-We also show that maxout polytopes are cubical for generic networks without bottlenecks.</p>
--
68-<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p2">
69-<p class="ltx_p">Under certain hypotheses, the tropical rational function is a tropical polynomial.
70-This means that the function is convex: it is the support function of
71-a convex polytope.
72:This is guaranteed if the neural network has only non-negative weights after the first layer, leading to so-called input convex neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#bib.bib2" title="">2</a>]</cite>.
73-We restrict to this case and, furthermore, consider
74:the maxout activation function instead of ReLU for technical reasons; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#bib.bib15" title="">15</a>, Remark 2.2]</cite>.</p>
75-</div>
76-<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p3">
77-<p class="ltx_p">As the weights of the network
78-vary, interesting classes of polytopes arise.
--
87-The purpose of this article is to study interesting polytopes arising in this recursive fashion, generalizing the class of zonotopes:</p>
88-</div>
89-<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p4">
90-<p class="ltx_p">We introduce <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maxout polytopes</span> and offer a detailed study of this family of polytopes with controlled depth.
91:Such a polytope is obtained from a feedforward
92:neural network with maxout activation function with two arguments,
93:where all weights after the first layer are non-negative.
94-See Section <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S2" title="2 From Networks to Polytopes ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for precise definitions.
95-Two <math alttext="3" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p4.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional maxout polytopes are depicted in
96-Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Maxout Polytopes"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Each diagram shows the convex hull of
97-two axis-parallel boxes in <math alttext="3" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p4.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-space.
--
200-<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
201-<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>From Networks to Polytopes</h2>
202-<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.p1">
203-<p class="ltx_p">We review some basics on ReLU neural networks and polytopes; for further details see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#bib.bib13" title="">13</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.21286v1#bib.bib29" title="">29</a>]</cite>.
204:Consider a function <math alttext="f" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p1.m1" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> from <math alttext="\mathbb{R}^{m_{0}}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p1.m2" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{m_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math alttext="\mathbb{R}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.p1.m3" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that is represented by a feedforward neural network</p>
